  "id": "sudanese_zar",
  "name": "Zar (Sudanese bori and tambura spirit ceremonies)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Spirit-possession healing ceremonies of Sudan, in which participants, most of them women, are brought into trance by songs addressed to particular spirits. Zar bori ceremonies are led by a female shaykha with a chorus of women over drums and percussion, while the tambura (tumbura) branch, kept by communities in Omdurman and Khartoum, centres on a male leader playing the large six-string tanbura lyre and a dancer wearing the mangur hoof-rattle belt. The membrane-drum card stands in for the dalluka and other drums.",
  "recipe": "classic modern zar, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. single leader group response, single lead single track no stacking. six string trance inducing tanbura kisir, mangour hoof rattle, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, hand clap. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
